ich details the required features for diagnosis [Data: Entities (79), Relationships (81, 87); Entities (28), Entities (84)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"The International Headache Classification is the authoritative source for diagnostic criteria of migraine without aura\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Migraine without aura is a subtype within the broader category of primary headache disorders, which are defined as headaches not caused by an underlying disease. This classification is important because it distinguishes migraine from secondary headaches, which result from conditions such as brain tumors or infections. The fact that migraine without aura falls under primary headache disorders underscores its significance in neurology and primary care, where it is frequently diagnosed and managed. The relationship between primary headache and migraine without aura highlights the hierarchical structure of headache classification, with migraine without aura being a specific, well-defined diagnosis within a larger diagnostic category [Data: Entities (75), Entities (28), Relationships (74)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Migraine without aura is a specific subtype within the broader category of primary headache disorders\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"General practice is the recommended clinical setting for the initial diagnosis of migraine without aura, serving as the first and second levels of diagnostic care. This reflects the high prevalence of the condition and the importance of early recognition in primary care settings. General practitioners are often the first point of contact for patients experiencing recurrent headaches, making them critical in identifying and managing migraine without aura. The relationship between migraine without aura and general practice emphasizes the role of frontline healthcare providers in the early detection and treatment of this common disorder [Data: Entities (78), Relationships (77)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"General practice is the recommended setting for the initial diagnosis of migraine without aura\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"For patients who meet most but not all criteria for migraine without aura—particularly those who have not yet experienced the required minimum number of attacks—a provisional diagnosis of probable migraine without aura is applied. This diagnostic category allows clinicians to identify and manage patients who are likely to develop full migraine without aura, even if they do not yet meet the full frequency threshold. The use of this provisional diagnosis supports early intervention and monitoring, potentially preventing progression to more severe or frequent attacks. The relationship between migraine without aura and probable migraine without aura reflects a clinical strategy for early identification and management [Data: Entities (80), Relationships (83)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Probable migraine without aura is a provisional diagnosis used when criteria are nearly met\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The diagnostic criteria for migraine without aura are modified for children and adolescents, with a shorter minimum duration requirement of 2 to 72 hours, compared to the standard 4 to 72 hours for adults. This adjustment acknowledges the physiological and developmental differences in headache presentation among younger populations. The shorter duration threshold ensures that pediatric patients are not underdiagnosed due to the natural variability in headache duration during early life stages. The relationship between migraine without aura and children and adolescents highlights the importance of age-specific diagnostic criteria in improving diagnostic accuracy in younger populations [Data: Entities (83), Relationships (86)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Diagnostic duration criteria for migraine without aura are adjusted for children and adolescents\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Migraine without aura and migraine with aura are distinct but related diagnostic subtypes of migraine that can co-occur in the same patient. According to diagnostic principles, a patient may experience both types of migraine, which are separately coded in the ICHD-3 classification. This co-occurrence is clinically significant because it affects treatment planning and patient management. The relationship between the two subtypes reflects the complexity and heterogeneity of migraine as a condition, requiring careful differentiation for accurate diagnosis and effective treatment [Data: Entities (28), Relationships (76)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Migraine without aura and migraine with aura are distinct but can co-occur in the same patient\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Table 4, referenced in the diagnostic criteria for migraine without aura, contains the formal diagnostic requirements used in clinical practice. This table is a key component of the IHC’s guidelines and is widely used by clinicians, researchers, and educators to standardize diagnosis. The inclusion of specific features such as headache duration, location, quality, and associated symptoms in Table 4 ensures consistency across different healthcare settings. The relationship between migraine without aura and Table 4 underscores the importance of standardized documentation in medical diagnosis [Data: Entities (84), Relationships (87)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Table 4 contains the formal diagnostic criteria for migraine without aura\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate to high due to the high prevalence of migraine without aura and its significant burden on healthcare systems and quality of life, despite being a non-life-threatening condition.\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community centers on migraine without aura, a common primary headache diso